QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=1851
Distance to LMC ≈ 160,000 light years (commonly ~159,000 ly, sometimes 163,000). Speed 0.99999987c → γ = 1/√(1-0.99999987²). 1-β² = (1-β)(1+β) ≈ 1.3e-7 × 2 = 2.6e-7. √ ≈ 5.099e-4. γ ≈ 1961.

Travel time in Earth frame: 160,000/0.99999987 ≈ 160,000 years. Astronaut time: 160,000/γ ≈ 81.6 years.

Hmm, with distance 163,000 ly: 163,000/1961 ≈ 83.1. With 159,000: 81.1. Answer choices: 81, 77, 72. Likely intended distance ~160,000 ly → 81.6 ≈ 81? Or maybe they use time dilation differently.

Actually maybe intended: LMC distance 159,000 ly. γ: compute more precisely. β = 0.99999987, 1-β = 1.3e-7. γ ≈ 1/√(2×1.3e-7) = 1/√(2.6e-7) = 1/(5.0990e-4) = 1961.2.

Proper time = distance/β/γ ≈ 159,000/1961 ≈ 81.1 years. So B: 81 years.

But wait — the astronaut is 22 years old; lifetime 150 years. 22+81 = 103 < 150, so astronaut survives. Answer B.</think>## Setting Up the Problem

**Distance:** The Large Magellanic Cloud is approximately 160,000 light-years from Earth (≈ 159,000 ly).

**Speed:** β = 0.99999987, so 1 − β = 1.3 × 10⁻⁷

## Lorentz Factor

$$\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\beta^2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{(1-\beta)(1+\beta)}}$$

$$\gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{(1.3\times10^{-7})(1.99999987)}} \approx \frac{1}{\sqrt{2.6\times10^{-7}}} \approx \frac{1}{5.099\times10^{-4}} \approx 1961$$

## Travel Time in the Astronaut's Frame

**Earth-frame travel time:**
$$t = \frac{d}{v} \approx \frac{159{,}000 \text{ ly}}{0.99999987\,c} \approx 159{,}000 \text{ years}$$

**Astronaut's (proper) time** — divide by γ:
$$\tau = \frac{t}{\gamma} \approx \frac{159{,}000}{1961} \approx 81 \text{ years}$$

## Checking Survival

The astronaut ages 81 years during the trip, so they arrive at age 22 + 81 = 103 years — well within the 150-year lifetime. The astronaut survives the journey.

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

