The key factor in selecting blasting charge: hardness of ore/rock (f). Common answer: ore hardness.</think>In mining blasting operations, the amount of explosive charge must be matched to how difficult it is to break the rock or ore. Harder, more competent rock requires more explosive energy per unit volume to fragment properly, while softer rock requires less. This property — the hardness (and associated drillability/blastability) of the ore — is the primary consideration when determining a reasonable charge amount. Other factors like ore grade, density, or mine depth may influence economics or design details, but they do not govern the charge size the way hardness does.

