Threshold: E γ ε ≳ (m_e c²)². E = (m_e c²)² / ε = (0.511 MeV)² / 10^-3 eV.

(5.11e5 eV)² = 2.61e11 eV². Divide by 1e-3 eV: 2.61e14 eV = 2.61e5 GeV... wait 2.61e14 eV = 2.61e8 GeV? No: 1 GeV = 1e9 eV. 2.61e14 eV = 2.61e5 GeV. Yes, 2.6e5 GeV.

Answer B.</think>**Threshold condition for pair production**

For γγ → e⁺e⁻ to occur, the center-of-mass energy must at least equal 2mₑc². This gives the threshold condition:

$$E_\gamma \, \epsilon \gtrsim (m_e c^2)^2$$

where ε is the target photon energy.

**Calculation**

With mₑc² = 0.511 MeV = 5.11 × 10⁵ eV and ε = 10⁻³ eV:

$$E_\gamma = \frac{(m_e c^2)^2}{\epsilon} = \frac{(5.11 \times 10^5 \text{ eV})^2}{10^{-3} \text{ eV}}$$

$$E_\gamma = \frac{2.61 \times 10^{11} \text{ eV}^2}{10^{-3} \text{ eV}} = 2.61 \times 10^{14} \text{ eV}$$

Converting to GeV (1 GeV = 10⁹ eV):

$$E_\gamma = 2.61 \times 10^5 \text{ GeV}$$

This matches choice B. Gamma rays above this energy are attenuated by pair production on the CMB, limiting their lifetime/distance of propagation in the universe.

Answer: B
