SNAr of aryl halides under alkaline conditions:
- Leaving group Cl. Rate enhanced by electron-withdrawing groups ortho/para to leaving group; nitro strongest. Electron-donating groups (NH2, NMe2, CH3) decrease.
- Multiple nitros at o/p increase.
- Steric hindrance by ortho substituents can reduce; but nitro groups are activating.
A: 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene: Cl has NO2 at ortho and para. Very high.
H: 1-chloro-2,6-dinitrobenzene: Cl has NO2 at both ortho. Very high but steric hindrance and "o-o" nitros twist; maybe lower than A.
C: 1,3-dichloro-2-nitrobenzene? Actually 2,6-dichloronitrobenzene = 1-nitro-2,6-dichlorobenzene. Leaving Cl at C2/C6 (ortho to NO2) activated by one ortho NO2, but also ortho Cl steric. Lower.
D: 1-nitro-2,4-dichlorobenzene. Leaving Cl at C4 para to NO2 (no ortho steric) or C2 ortho. Para is more reactive than C? D maybe high but one NO2.
B: Cl at C2, NO2 para, NH2 ortho. NH2 donates, but NO2 withdraws. Could be moderate.
G: Cl at C3, NO2 ortho, NH2 meta. Moderate; no deactivation by amino (meta). Could be more reactive than B? Maybe.
F: Cl para to NMe2, deactivated.
E: if aryl Cl? "3-nitro-4-chloromethylbenzeneamine" maybe maybe "3-nitro-4-chloro methylbenzeneamine" (methylbenzeneamine = toluidine) with chloro and nitro substituents? But "chloromethyl" maybe not.

