Count subsets of 16 chairs of size 8 with no three consecutive occupied. N = number of binary strings length 16 with 8 ones, no 111.

Compute: number of strings with 8 ones, no 3 consecutive. Use runs of ones: split 8 ones into runs of size 1 or 2, separated by at least one zero. Number of runs k, zeros: 16-8=8 zeros, need k-1 zeros as separators, remaining 8-(k-1)=9-k zeros distributed freely (before first run, after last, extra in gaps).

Runs sizes: each run 1 or 2, sum 8, k runs: number of compositions of 8 into k parts each ≤2 = C(k, 8-k)... standard: number of ways = C(k, 8-k). Let me check: parts of size 1 or 2 summing to 8 with k parts: number of 2s = 8-k, choose positions: C(k, 8-k).

Arrangement of runs among ones and zeros: total positions = k runs + (9-k) free zeros = k + 9 - k = 9 slots... Actually the count: choose which of the k-1 gaps get the extra zeros: distribute 9-k zeros into k+1 slots (before, after, k-1 gaps): number of ways = C(9-k + k, k) = C(9, k)? Number of nonneg solutions of x_0+...+x_k = 9-k is C(9-k+k, k) = C(9, k).

So N = Σ_k C(k, 8-k) C(9, k), where k ranges with 8-k ≤ k, i.e., k ≥ 4, and k ≤ 8.

k=4: C(4,4)C(9,4)=1·126=126
k=5: C(5,3)C(9,5)=10·126=1260
k=6: C(6,2)C(9,6)=15·84=1260
k=7: C(7,1)C(9,7)=7·36=252
