ΔG°f(Fe2O3) = -177 kcal/mol (per mole of Fe2O3, since reaction 1 forms 1 mol Fe2O3 from elements).

Reaction 2: 4 Fe2O3 + Fe → 3 Fe3O4, ΔG = -19 kcal.

ΔG = 3ΔGf(Fe3O4) - 4(-177) = -19
3ΔGf(Fe3O4) + 708 = -19
3ΔGf = -727
ΔGf = -242.33 kcal/mol.

Answer D.</think>## Setting Up

**Given:**
- Reaction (1): 2Fe(s) + 3/2 O₂(g) → Fe₂O₃(s), ΔG° = −177 kcal

Since this reaction forms 1 mol of Fe₂O₃ from elements in their standard states:
$$\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_2O_3) = -177 \text{ kcal/mol}$$

- Reaction (2): 4Fe₂O₃(s) + Fe(s) → 3Fe₃O₄(s), ΔG° = −19 kcal

## Applying Hess's Law

For reaction (2):
$$\Delta G^\circ_{rxn} = 3\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_3O_4) - 4\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_2O_3)$$

(Fe(s) is an element, so its ΔG°f = 0.)

Substituting:
$$-19 = 3\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_3O_4) - 4(-177)$$

$$-19 = 3\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_3O_4) + 708$$

$$3\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_3O_4) = -19 - 708 = -727$$

$$\Delta G_f^\circ(Fe_3O_4) = \frac{-727}{3} = -242.33 \text{ kcal/mol}$$

This matches choice **D** (−242.3 kcal/mol).

Answer: D
